{"text": "Nicotine & Tobacco Research, 2015, 1255-1262\ndoi:10.1093/ntr/ntu333 1\nOriginal investigation |\nAdvance Access publication January 7, 2015 |\n\nOriginal investigation\n\nThe Impact of Flavor Descriptors on\nNonsmoking Teens\u2019 and Adult Smokers\u2019 Interest\nin Electronic Cigarettes\n\nSaul Shiffman PhD\u201d, Mark A. Sembower MS\", Janine L. Pillitteri PhD\u2019,\nKaren K. Gerlach PhD, MPH\", Joseph G. Gitchell BA\u00ae\n\n\u2018Pinney Associates, Pittsburgh, PA; Department of Psychology, University of Pittsburgh, Pittsburgh, PA; 3Pinney\nAssociates, Bethesda, MD\n\nCorresponding Author: Saul Shiffman, PhD, Pinney Associates, 201 North Craig Street, Suite 320, Pittsburgh, PA 15213,\nUSA. Telephone: 412-567-1761; Fax: 412-687-5677; E-mail: shitfman@pinneyassociates.com\n\nAbstract\n\nIntroduction: Smokers switching completely from combustible cigarettes to electronic cigarettes\n(e-cigarettes) are likely to reduce health risk, suggesting that e-cigarettes should be made appeal-\ning to adult smokers. However, uptake of e-cigarettes by nonsmoking teens would add risk with-\nout benefit and should be avoided. Although e-cigarette flavors may appeal to adult smokers, the\nconcern is that flavors might attract nonsmoking teens.\nMethods: Nonsmoking teens (n = 216, ages 13-17, no tobacco in past 6 months) and adult smok-\ners (n = 432, ages 19-80, smoking 3+ years; could have used e-cigarettes) were recruited from an\nInternet research panel. In assessments completed online (May 22, 2014 to June 13, 2014), par\nticipants indicated their interest (0-10 scale) in e-cigarettes paired with various flavor descriptors.\nThese were mixed (order balanced) with similar flavor offerings for ice cream and bottled water\nto mask the focus on e-cigarettes and validate the assessment. Mixed models contrasted interest\nbetween teens and adults and among adults by e-cigarette history.\nResults: Nonsmoking teens\u2019 interest in e-cigarettes was very low (mean = 0.41+0.14 [SE] on 0-10\nscale), Adult smokers\u2019 interest (1.73+0,10), while modest, was significantly higher overall (p <\n.0001) and for each flavor (most p values < .0001), Teen interest did not vary by flavor (p = .75),\nbut adult interest did (p < .0001). Past-30-day adult e-cigarette users had the greatest interest in\ne-cigarettes, and their interest was most affected by flavor. Adults who never tried e-cigarettes had\nthe lowest interest, yet still higher than nonsmoking teens\u2019 interest (p < .0001).\nConclusion: The e-cigarette flavors tested appealed more to adult smokers than to nonsmoking\nteens, but interest in flavors was low for both groups.\n\nIntroduction If current smokers switched to nicotine-only products such as e-ciga-\n\nThe effect of el G5 t bie: haaltin te bed rettes, it is projected that harm would be dramatically reduced.\u201d\npeda S ois Paes ese) PRERA : Be The public health debate, however, centers on the effects on the\nhotly debated. Although nicotine itself is not harmless, it is known sible population, which fa the seanelavdl eatudiliahed duc thle ior tha\n\nthat the harm of smoking overwhelmingly comes from the products gave the Food and Drug Administration regulatory authority over\n\nS10 \u2018LT Jequiaydag uo ssacov aquiat CoDeqQO,| PUR aLNOOIN UO YoIRasaY Joy ASto0g Je /J10's;eumolpiojxo'Hu//:dyY Woy papeojUMog,\n\nof tobacco combustion.' Accordingly, it has been proposed that e-ciga-\nrettes have the potential to dramatically decrease the risk to individuals\nwho completely switch to them from smoking combustible cigarettes.**\n\ntobacco,\u2019 and this depends heavily on who adopts e-cigarettes.?\nUptake of e-cigarettes by individuals who would not otherwise\nsmoke does not decrease risk and could increase it. This could occur\n\n\u00a9 The Author 2015. Published by Oxford University Press on behalf of the Society for Research on Nicotine and Tobacco. Alll rights reserved. 1255,\n\nFor permissions, please e-mail: journals,pe